<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The framework contains ARM64 code, yet Xcode refuses it for an Apple-silicon simulator. CPU architecture is only one part of an artifact’s identity. The source graph, target platform and packaged slices must agree.</p>

Trace the build inputs to the consumer artifact before changing source folders or copying another project’s build script.

<!-- @after Why Device ARM64 and Simulator ARM64 Differ :: visual -->
### Same instruction family, different destinations

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong>Device compilation</strong><small>iosArm64 and its device SDK/platform identity</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Simulator compilation</strong><small>iosSimulatorArm64 and its simulator identity</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Framework slice</strong><small>Binary plus the API and metadata for one destination</small></div><div class="node"><strong>XCFramework</strong><small>Packages the slices a consumer is allowed to select</small></div></div><figcaption>A folder name or the word ARM64 alone is insufficient evidence that the consumer selected the right artifact.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after How Variant-Aware Dependency Resolution Works :: animation -->
### Resolve for the actual consumer

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Resolve for the actual consumer</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Declaration</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>Consumer target</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Published variants</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Selection</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Declare", "caption": "A common dependency expresses a coordinate and version. It does not turn every artifact at that coordinate into native code.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Dependency requested", "v1": "Not one universal target", "v2": "Producer metadata", "v3": "Not selected"}}, {"label": "Request", "caption": "The target compilation supplies its required attributes. Android and Apple compilations can request different variants.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Same declared library", "v1": "Concrete compilation", "v2": "Candidate artifacts", "v3": "Match required"}}, {"label": "Match", "caption": "Gradle chooses a compatible published variant. Missing target support is a resolution problem to investigate, not a reason to move a JVM JAR into a new folder.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Common API intent", "v1": "Target constraints", "v2": "Matching or missing", "v3": "Diagnostic evidence"}}, {"label": "Consume", "caption": "Compile/link the selected artifact and verify the final application integration. Exported Swift declarations are a separate public-surface decision.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Scope remains explicit", "v1": "Build the target", "v2": "Exact version retained", "v3": "Test the consumer"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform/multiplatform-hierarchy.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Convention Plugin Design :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Does an XCFramework merge device and simulator into one interchangeable binary?</summary><p>It packages distinct slices so the build can select the correct one.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does api automatically export all dependency declarations to Swift?</summary><p>Kotlin dependency visibility and native framework export are distinct decisions.</p></details>
<details><summary>What proves a dependency supports every target?</summary><p>Inspect the exact published variants and compile/test the required consumer matrix.</p></details>

<!-- @diagram f30234d48041765e -->
### Who can see whose code

<figure class="viz"><div class="node is-key" style="max-width:24rem;margin:0 auto .6rem"><strong><code>commonMain</code></strong><small>APIs available to every target</small></div><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong><code>androidMain</code></strong><small>Android/JVM APIs</small></div><div class="col"><div class="node"><strong><code>iosMain</code></strong><small>APIs common to the selected iOS targets</small></div><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-state="ghost"><strong><code>iosArm64Main</code></strong><small>device-specific escape hatch</small></div><div class="node" data-state="ghost"><strong><code>iosSimulatorArm64Main</code></strong><small>simulator-specific escape hatch</small></div></div></div></div><figcaption>Redrawn from the original diagram. A source set sees declarations from the sets above it, never from below: <code>iosMain</code> can call <code>commonMain</code>, but <code>commonMain</code> cannot call <code>iosMain</code>.</figcaption></figure>
